# YouTube local v1 experimental scaffold

Generated by Jeeves from frozen snapshot005. This project is independent of the Jeeves checkout.
Read `.jeeves/report.json` for every setting, unsupported override and G01–G08. This is partial,
unverified application scaffolding. Shell health checks establish process/transport readiness
only; business routes fail with 501 and worker messages fail into bounded retry/failed queues.
No real encoding, upload lifecycle, public publication, search or view-count behavior is supplied.

Requirements: Linux amd64 Docker Compose, Node 24.21.0 and pnpm 12.16.1. OpenSearch needs
`vm.max_map_count >= 262144`, roughly 1 GiB heap plus native memory; check host prerequisites
without automatically changing sysctl. All browser ports bind loopback (4400–4402). No cloud.

```
pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
pnpm build
node scripts/setup.mjs
docker compose build
docker compose up -d --wait postgres cache broker objects search-index identity
node scripts/bootstrap.mjs
docker compose run --rm broker-init
docker compose run --rm search-init
docker compose up -d --wait watch search upload transcoder view-counter completion-handler search-indexer edge
```

Setup creates private `.runtime` secrets once; never commit it or discard it while keeping volumes.
Creator username is `creator`; read its password from `.runtime/secrets/creator_password` locally.
Open http://127.0.0.1:4400 for the scaffold notice. Keycloak uses public issuer
http://127.0.0.1:4401/realms/youtube-v1 and PKCE browser client `youtube-v1-web`, API audience
`youtube-v1-api`. JWT helper verifies that public issuer using internal JWKS; custom routes must
invoke it. No browser auth flow has been implemented. Anonymous originals must remain private.

`docker compose down` retains data; only use `down -v` for a disposable instance whose data you own.
Choose a unique `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` for simultaneous copies; browser ports still conflict.
Bootstrap is deliberately single-use and refuses existing keys. If it fails part way, preserve
runtime/data and inspect the error; no reset/recovery/credential rotation automation is supplied.

PG grants isolate metadata/counts/identity; only completion-handler receives metadata owner and
only view-counter receives counts owner. Upload/transcoder receive no metadata access. Custom
migrations must run as the matching owner for default reader grants to apply. Garage keys grant
bucket access, not per-object creator ownership; upload ownership/resume is custom G01/G06.
Transcoder reads originals and writes transcoded. Fanout subscribers are independent; there is
no committed-metadata-before-search ordering guarantee (G02), no outbox or deduplication (G03).
Retries confirm a copied message before ack and may duplicate on crash. Failed queues are visible;
no automated re-drive or cross-process exactly-once guarantee. Batching/cache-aside/single-flight,
HTTP execution deadlines and admission limits remain custom even when values are in config.

FFmpeg uses Debian snapshots dated 20261024T000000Z with exact 7:5.1.9-0+deb12u1. The Docker
build checks the deb SHA256 and exact resolved OS package versions against `infra/ffmpeg-os.lock`.
The Node base image, all resource images and npm closure are pinned. This does not promise
bit-identical image builds, cross-platform support, HA, production security, TLS/mTLS, CDN scale,
throughput or a passing S01–S08 scenario. S3 adapter explicitly chooses WHEN_REQUIRED checksum
modes for Garage's subset; no Amazon S3 parity is asserted. Public signing uses its own endpoint;
never rewrite the signed Host. CORS allows exact web origin and exposes ETag.

Extension points: `src/extensions/`, HTTP shell files in `src/services/`, worker shell files in
`src/workers/`. All initial files remain generated scaffold/extension, not custom completion.
Do not regenerate over edits. Consult `.jeeves/manifest.json` for hashes/provenance and immutable
source/image inputs. hls.js is locked for later UI integration; its presence supplies no playback.
